course: geography
lesson: 36
title: The people who were already there: what colonisation did
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Europa Universalis 5 often paints the Americas, Australia and much of Africa as empty land waiting for colonists, but every valley and coast already had people with their own nations, laws, farms and cities.
explain: Imagine new neighbours moving into your street and announcing that your house is "unclaimed" because they had not seen it before: that is how colonisers often treated land that was already someone's home.
explain: After 1492 smallpox, measles and flu crossed the Atlantic and killed far more people than swords did; American peoples had never met these diseases, so their bodies had no defence against them, no immunity.
explain: Then came forced labour, broken treaties and the taking of land, in the Americas, Australia, New Zealand and Africa, and many historians call the collapse in the Americas one of the largest in human history.
explain: But this is not only history: about 476 million Indigenous people live today, and many are bringing back their languages, rights and lands.
fact: When Europeans arrived in 1492, tens of millions of people lived in the Americas, in cities, farming villages and many nations.
fact: Within about a century after 1492, the Indigenous population of the Americas fell by perhaps 80 to 90 percent; historians still argue about the exact numbers.
fact: Far more deadly diseases travelled from Europe to the Americas than the other way, because Europeans had long lived close to farm animals and crowded towns where such diseases arose.
fact: Under the Spanish encomienda system, colonists were given the right to the labour and tribute of Indigenous people, and the friar Bartolomé de las Casas wrote fiercely against its cruelty.
fact: North America held many Indigenous nations, such as the Haudenosaunee, Cherokee, Lakota and Powhatan, who made treaties, traded and fought with Europeans.
fact: The Haudenosaunee Confederacy, also called the Iroquois, joined five, later six, nations in a lasting alliance.
fact: In the 1830s the USA forced the Cherokee and other nations of the south-east off their lands to the west, and thousands died on the march known as the Trail of Tears.
fact: Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have lived in Australia for at least 50,000 to 65,000 years, among the oldest continuing cultures in the world.
fact: When Britain began a colony in Australia in 1788 it treated the land as belonging to no one, an idea Australia's High Court rejected in the Mabo decision of 1992.
fact: British colonisation brought disease, violent frontier killings and the taking of land to Aboriginal peoples, and later many Aboriginal children were taken from their families; they are called the Stolen Generations.
fact: In 1840 many Māori chiefs and the British Crown signed the Treaty of Waitangi, but the English and Māori texts said different things about who held power.
fact: In the 1800s and early 1900s European rule in Africa often meant taking land, forced labour and violent punishment of resistance; in King Leopold II's Congo Free State forced rubber collection caused millions of deaths.
fact: Indigenous peoples are still here, about 476 million people across about 90 countries, and many are reviving their languages, rights and land; the UN adopted a Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples in 2007.
exercise: Were the Americas empty when Europeans arrived in 1492? | No, tens of millions of people lived there | Yes, nobody at all lived there before Columbus arrived | Only a few hundred hunters lived there | Only the Caribbean islands had any people living on them
exercise: By about how much did the Indigenous population of the Americas fall within a century after 1492? | Perhaps 80 to 90 percent | By about 5 percent | About half a percent | It grew by about a quarter instead
exercise: Why did so many Indigenous people die after 1492? | New diseases such as smallpox, plus war and forced labour | Almost all of them sailed away to Europe and never came back | A huge earthquake hit both continents | They stopped growing food on purpose
exercise: Why did American peoples have no immunity to smallpox? | The disease had never existed in the Americas before | They refused to wash their hands with soap | They lived too high in the mountains for medicine to reach them | Smallpox only harms people who eat maize
exercise: Which way did far more deadly diseases travel? | From Europe to the Americas | From the Americas to Europe | Equally both ways | From Australia to Europe
exercise: What did the encomienda give Spanish colonists? | The right to the labour and tribute of Indigenous people | Free land back home in Spain, given by the king to every settler | A ship to sail home | A seat in the Pope's council
exercise: Who was Bartolomé de las Casas? | A friar who wrote against the cruelty of the encomienda | The last Aztec emperor, who fought Cortés | The captain of a Manila galleon | A king of Portugal who sent ships to Brazil
exercise: Which is a North American Indigenous nation? | The Cherokee | The Taíno of Cuba | The Māori of New Zealand | The Manchus of China
exercise: What was the Haudenosaunee Confederacy? | An alliance of five, later six, nations | A Spanish trading company in Mexico | A British fort on the St Lawrence in Canada | A long canoe made of birch bark
exercise: What was the Trail of Tears? | The forced removal of the Cherokee and others in the 1830s | A long trade route for furs across Siberia to China and Japan | A river in Australia | A Spanish road in Mexico
exercise: How long have Aboriginal peoples lived in Australia? | At least 50,000 to 65,000 years | About 500 years, since the 1500s | Only since the first British ships came in 1788 | About 2,000 years, since Roman times
exercise: What did the Mabo decision of 1992 reject? | The idea that Australia had belonged to no one | The right of Australia to have its own flag and anthem | The Treaty of Waitangi | The building of Sydney Opera House
exercise: Who are the Stolen Generations? | Aboriginal children taken from their families by governments | Sailors who stole ships from Britain | Convicts sent to Australia from Britain for stealing food | Children who ran away to sea
exercise: Why do the two versions of the Treaty of Waitangi matter? | The English and Māori texts said different things about power | They were signed in different years by different people | One was written in French | They are exactly the same, word for word, in both languages
exercise: What happened in King Leopold II's Congo Free State? | Forced rubber collection caused millions of deaths | It became the richest free country in the whole of Africa | It was ruled by its own elected kings and queens | It was never colonised by anyone at all
exercise: About how many Indigenous people live in the world today? | About 476 million | About 476 thousand | None, they all died out | About 4 billion people
exercise: What did the UN adopt in 2007? | A Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples | The Treaty of Tordesillas | A ban on all borders between countries in the world | The Berlin Conference rules
test: In EU5 you "colonise an empty province" in North America. What really happened in such places? | Colonists took land that was already someone's home | The land was truly empty, with no one living on it | Colonists bought every acre at a fair price | Indigenous peoples asked to be ruled
test: my mate says the Spanish won cos guns are op. that the main reason? | No, disease killed far more, and they had many Indigenous allies | Yes, guns alone killed nearly everyone, there was no other reason at all | Yes, Indigenous peoples had no weapons at all | No, the Indigenous peoples simply left
test: If American peoples had lived for centuries with smallpox, what would most likely have been different? | Far fewer would have died after 1492 | Columbus would have turned back | Europeans would have caught smallpox instead | Nothing at all would have changed
test: A Cherokee family in Georgia in 1838 is forced to march west by soldiers. What is this? | The Trail of Tears | The encomienda system | The Columbian Exchange | The devshirme levy
test: A friend says Australia's history starts in 1788. What is wrong? | Aboriginal peoples had lived there for tens of thousands of years | Nothing, nobody lived there before | Australia was only found in 1900, by ships from Japan and China | It started in 1492 with Columbus
test: A Māori chief signed a text saying chiefs keep their authority, while the English text said the Crown got sovereignty. What problem does this cause? | Each side believed it had agreed to something different | None, both texts said exactly the same thing in two languages | The treaty was about Australia | The chiefs could not sign at all
test: ugh, history's all done n dusted, Indigenous peoples aren't around anymore, right? | Wrong, about 476 million live today in about 90 countries | Right, they all died out by 1900 | Right, only a few hundred are left, all of them in museums | Wrong, but only in Europe
test: Why did diseases travel mostly from Europe to the Americas and not back? | Many came from living near farm animals and crowded towns, more common in Europe | American peoples were cleaner and never got ill | Ships could only carry germs one way, from east to west with the trade winds | Germs cannot live in the Americas
test: Which nations did the Haudenosaunee Confederacy join? | Five, later six, Indigenous nations | Spain and Portugal in one Catholic alliance | The Aztec and Inca empires of the south | All thirteen of the British colonies
test: A Spanish settler is given a village whose people must work for him. Which system is this? | The encomienda | The Commonwealth | The devshirme levy | The Treaty of Waitangi
test: If the Congo Free State had been run for its people, not for rubber profits, what would most likely have been different? | Millions of deaths from forced labour might not have happened | The Congo River would have dried up | Leopold II would have ruled Spain and Portugal as well as Belgium | Africa would have had no borders
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Americas at the time of European arrival in 1492? | The continents were inhabited by tens of millions of persons in numerous nations | The continents were wholly uninhabited | The continents were peopled solely by itinerant hunters numbering some hundreds | The continents were already Spanish possessions
exam: Identify the principal cause of the collapse of the Indigenous population of the Americas subsequent to 1492. | Introduced diseases, compounded by warfare and forced labour | An extended period of drought, without any other cause | Voluntary emigration to Europe | A decline in the birth rate unconnected with conquest
exam: Ascertain the significance of the Mabo decision rendered by the High Court of Australia in 1992. | It rejected the doctrine that the land had belonged to no one | It established the Commonwealth of Australia | It ratified the Treaty of Waitangi | It abolished the Australian monarchy and established a republic
exam: State the approximate present number of Indigenous persons in the world. | Approximately four hundred and seventy-six million | Approximately four hundred and seventy-six thousand | None whatsoever | Approximately four billion
